I don't think we can just inherit this from Token2.0 because removing a token from the cache is different for Gargle2.0 tokens. This is also not going to come up very often, i.e. is not urgent, but is worth doing.

relevant excerpt from `httr::Token2.0`:

``` r
  refresh = function() {
    cred <- refresh_oauth2.0(self$endpoint, self$app,
        self$credentials, self$params$user_params, self$params$use_basic_auth)
    if (is.null(cred)) {
      remove_cached_token(self)   # <-- this won't actually un-cache a Gargle2.0 token
    } else {
      self$credentials <- cred
      self$cache()
    }
    self
  },
```

During a r-lib/targets pull request review I had feedback to make the error conditions specific to the HTTP response type, and now its done I find it very helpful when building the Google API responses since you can do logic based on specific status code.

For example, creating a BigQuery table gives a 409 status error code if it already exists.  This can be parsed via tryCatch similar to:

```r
tryCatch(
    bqr_create_table(
      tableId = tableId,
      timePartitioning = TRUE
    ),
 error_409 = function(err){
    message("Table already exists")
  },
 error = function(err){
    stop(err$message)
})
```

This is nicer than before checking for the status code in the response myself etc.

I put this in `googleAuthR` using `rlang` via:

```r
abort_http <- function(status_code, msg = NULL){
  rlang::abort(paste0("http_",status_code), 
               message = paste0("http_", status_code, " ", msg)
  )
}
```

